---
import Learn from '../../layouts/Learn.astro';
import MemoryFlow from '../../components/learn/MemoryFlow.astro';
import BarChart from '../../components/learn/BarChart.astro';
import { COMPANY, PRICES, TRIAL } from '../../data/site';

// Learn comparison page 6 (s122; founder: Google's AI named Membase among
// Zaaheen's competitors). Rules: see ai-memory-apps-compared.astro and
// data/pricing.ts. Translated into de/es/fr/pt (in i18n TRANSLATED). Facts
// read on 2026-11-11:
// - membase.com: "the memory layer for AI agents and the people who use them";
//   built by Unibase, "not affiliated with membase.so"; Chrome extension for
//   ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Grok, DeepSeek and Kimi; Claude Code, Cursor and
//   Codex through its MCP server; desktop "Coming soon"; "The hosted service
//   is not end-to-end encrypted"; SDKs, CLI and MCP server on GitHub, "The core
//   memory engine is proprietary and ships as a compiled package". No prices.
// - membase.com/privacy (updated 2026-11-09): "Data is stored and processed on
//   Amazon Web Services in Singapore (ap-southeast-1)"; content is sent to "the
//   model provider we use (currently OpenAI)"; "encrypted at rest".
// - github.com/unibaseio/membase: local mode `pip install
//   'unibaseio-membase[local]'`, "Python 3.12 or 3.13 · needs OPENAI_API_KEY",
//   memory in ~/.membase; MIT for the SDK, the compiled engine under its own
//   Unibase licence; 140 stars.
// Chart: the 2026-06-05 Cursor test, as on the other comparison pages.
const CHECKED = '11 November 2026';
const OUTLINE = [
  { id: 'what', title: 'What Membase is' },
  { id: 'differ', title: 'Where Zaaheen differs' },
  { id: 'table', title: 'Side by side' },
  { id: 'better', title: 'Where Membase is the better choice' },
  { id: 'limits', title: 'Where Zaaheen falls short' },
];
const MB_HOME = 'https://membase.com/';
const MB_PRIVACY = 'https://membase.com/privacy';
const MB_GITHUB = 'https://github.com/unibaseio/membase';
---
<Learn
  path="/learn/membase-alternative/"
  lede="Membase is a hosted memory for AI apps and agents, with a browser extension, an MCP server and tools for developers. Its own site says the hosted service is not end-to-end encrypted. If you want one memory for ChatGPT, Claude and Cursor that stays encrypted on your own computer and needs no API key, Zaaheen is built for that instead."
  outline={OUTLINE}
>
  <section id="what">
    <h2>What Membase is</h2>
    <p>This page is about Membase at membase.com, made by a company called Unibase. A different
       product, at membase.so, also uses the name; the two are not connected
       (<a href={MB_HOME}>Membase's site</a>).</p>
    <p>Membase builds one long-term memory from your chats and files. Its browser extension works
       with ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Grok, DeepSeek and Kimi in Chrome, and Claude Code, Cursor and
       Codex connect through its MCP server. Developers can also use it from code. A desktop app is
       listed as coming soon (<a href={MB_HOME}>its home page</a>).</p>
    <p>Your memory is kept on Membase's service, which its privacy policy says runs on Amazon Web
       Services in Singapore, and the relevant parts of what you save are sent to its AI provider,
       currently OpenAI (<a href={MB_PRIVACY}>its privacy policy</a>). There is also a local mode for
       people who use Python, which keeps the memory in a folder on your computer but needs your own
       OpenAI API key (<a href={MB_GITHUB}>its GitHub page</a>).</p>
  </section>

  <section id="differ">
    <h2>Where Zaaheen differs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Encrypted on your computer, by default.</strong> Zaaheen keeps your memories
          encrypted on your own computer, and our servers never receive them. Membase's hosted
          service, its default, is not end-to-end encrypted, in its own words.</li>
      <li><strong>No API key, no outside AI service.</strong> Zaaheen runs its own AI models on your
          computer, so your memories are never sent to an AI company to be sorted or searched.
          Membase's local mode still needs an OpenAI key.</li>
      <li><strong>A finished app for Windows and Mac.</strong> Zaaheen installs like any other app,
          and you connect each AI app from its Agents tab. Membase's desktop app is not out yet.</li>
      <li><strong>Code you can read.</strong> All of Zaaheen's code is public to read, so anyone can
          check how your memories are handled. Membase publishes its tools, but its memory engine
          ships as a closed, compiled package.</li>
    </ul>

    <MemoryFlow
      id="flow-membase"
      from="Claude"
      to="Cursor"
      fact="Prefers TypeScript"
      others={['ChatGPT', 'Codex']}
      caption="Something you tell Claude is saved once to the encrypted memory on your computer, and Cursor and your other apps can recall it later."
    />
  </section>

  <section id="table">
    <h2>Side by side</h2>
    <table class="learn-table" tabindex="0">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Membase</th><th scope="col">Zaaheen</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Where memories are kept</th><td>Its hosted service in Singapore; or a local folder with the Python local mode</td><td>Your computer, encrypted</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Encryption</th><td>Encrypted at rest; the hosted service is not end-to-end encrypted, in its own words</td><td>Encrypted on your computer; our servers never receive your memories</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Sorted and searched by</th><td>OpenAI, hosted or local</td><td>AI models running on your computer</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Setup</th><td>Browser extension, MCP server, or Python for local mode</td><td>An installer, then connect each app</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Desktop app</th><td>Coming soon</td><td>Windows and Mac</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Price</th><td>No prices on its site when we checked</td><td>{TRIAL.days} days free, then ${PRICES.monthly} a month or ${PRICES.yearly} a year</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="learn-note">As of {CHECKED}, from Membase's own pages, and may change. Check the
       links above for the latest.</p>
    <p>What matters most in any memory is whether the right answer comes back, and whether the app
       admits it when the memory doesn't hold one. We asked Cursor five questions about facts another
       app had saved to a shared Zaaheen memory.</p>

    <BarChart
      id="chart-membase-cursor"
      title="5 questions to Cursor about facts saved by another app"
      measure="Questions"
      max={5}
      bars={[
        { label: 'Recalled the fact', value: 3, note: 'of 5' },
        { label: "Said it didn't know", value: 1, note: 'of 5' },
        { label: 'Avoided the trick', value: 1, note: 'of 5' },
      ]}
      source={{ text: "Zaaheen's own test, 5 June 2026, a development version of Zaaheen on Windows. Didn't know, correctly: a blood type the memory didn't hold. The trick: asked about the person's cat, it said they have a dog." }}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="better">
    <h2>Where Membase is the better choice</h2>
    <p>If you mostly use AI in a web browser, Membase's extension reaches ChatGPT, Gemini, Grok and
       others there, which Zaaheen doesn't. It can also build memory from files and pages you save,
       and developers get an API and tools to build with. And if you're happy for your memory to live
       online, it follows you to any computer.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="limits">
    <h2>Where Zaaheen falls short</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It runs on Windows and on Macs with Apple chips only, with no Linux or phone version.</li>
      <li>ChatGPT connects through the ChatGPT app for your computer, not in a web browser.</li>
      <li>Your memory lives on one computer; there is no sync between computers yet.</li>
      <li>It isn't open source, and after the {TRIAL.days}-day trial there is no free plan.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="learn-note">Membase is a name of its owner. We have no link with Membase or Unibase.
       Facts checked on {CHECKED}; if anything here is wrong or out of date, tell us at
       <a href={`mailto:${COMPANY.email}`}>{COMPANY.email}</a> and we'll correct it.</p>
  </section>
</Learn>
